comments_list.body:
addressPrefixes is required parameter in br/public:avm/res/network/virtual-network:0.7.2. Removing the explicit CIDR is not possible in the AVM module. 

https://github.com/Azure/bicep-registry-modules/tree/main/avm/res/network/virtual-network#parameter-addressprefixes

This code updates the vnet and link it to IPAM pool:

resource existingVnet 'Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks@2024-05-01' = {
  name: 'vnet-test'
  location: 'westeurope'
  properties: {
    addressSpace: {
      addressPrefixes: [
        '10.0.0.0/24'
      ]
      ipamPoolPrefixAllocations: [
        {
          pool: {
            id: '/subscriptions/***/resourceGroups/myvnet/providers/Microsoft.Network/networkManagers/mymanager/ipamPools/pool1'
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}

> [@janinternet](https://github.com/janinternet) I have been playing with this kind of use cases. What I found so far is that
> 
> * You cannot provide both explicit CIDR and IPAM pool configuration at the same time in Bicep Vnet resource.
> * If vnet and ip pool exists that has available CIDR (same used in vnet), then you can update the bicep template to just use the IPAM config (removing the explicit CIDR) which did allocate the same CIDR range from the pool. Thus transition was smooth. Alternatively you could associate the vnet manually in the portal to a ip pool then update the template to just use the pool configuration and apply (I expect no change in -whatIf).
> * Associating a vnet to IP Pools a an update to VNET and you dont have control on that then you could go with Static CDIR pool which is a metadata update to the AVNM thus reserving that IP range from ever allocating to another VNET.
> 
> Also from Bicep I dont know how to add a secondary cidr to a VNET under IPAM management which is different use case.

@jeevanions When removing the addressPrefixes, the already assigned addresses are indeed preserved by IPAM.

The issue is that we also want to be able to delete and redeploy virtual networks with the same address space.
This means we need to be able to manually define an address space but still register it to IPAM simultaneously.
What we require is a separate parameter to specify the IPAM Pool ID on top of the VNET addressPrefixes. 

The below code is what we want, but you have made it so there must be a pool OR addressPrefixes for the VNET.
Yet for the subnet you do allow both fields to be entered. That is precisely what we wish to have changed.
```bicep annotate module virtualNetwork 'br/public:avm/res/network/virtual-network:0.9.0' = {
  params: {
    // Required parameters
    name: 'VN-Shared-DEV'
    addressPrefixes: [
      '10.31.254.128/26'
    ]
    ipamPoolPrefixAllocations: [
      {
        pool: {
          id: '/subscriptions/***/resourceGroups/RG-IPAM-DEV/providers/Microsoft.Network/networkManagers/NM-SVH-DEV/ipamPools/IP-Pool01-DEV'
        }
      }
    ]
    // Non-required parameters
    subnets: [
      {
        name: 'SN-Shared-Keyvault-DEV'
        addressPrefix: '10.31.254.128/29'
        ipamPoolPrefixAllocations: [
          {
            pool: {
              id: '/subscriptions/***/resourceGroups/RG-IPAM-DEV/providers/Microsoft.Network/networkManagers/NM-SVH-DEV/ipamPools/IP-Pool01-DEV'
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

The native Microsoft module already has support for this feature, the below code was successfully deployed.

```bicep annotate resource virtualNetwork 'Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks@2025-07-01' = {
  name: 'VN-Shared-DEV'
  location: resourceGroup().location
  properties: {
    addressSpace: {
      addressPrefixes: [
        '10.31.254.128/26'
      ]
      ipamPoolPrefixAllocations: [
        {
          pool: {
            id: '/subscriptions/***/resourceGroups/RG-IPAM-DEV/providers/Microsoft.Network/networkManagers/NM-SVH-DEV/ipamPools/IP-Pool01-DEV'
          }
        }
      ]
    }
    subnets: [
      {
        name: 'SN-Shared-Keyvault-DEV'
        properties: {
          addressPrefix: '10.31.254.128/29'
          ipamPoolPrefixAllocations: [
            {
              pool: {
                id: '/subscriptions/***/resourceGroups/RG-IPAM-DEV/providers/Microsoft.Network/networkManagers/NM-SVH-DEV/ipamPools/IP-Pool01-DEV'
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

@AlexanderSehr actually in our module we were only adding the secrets to an existing vault. This seems to be an issue with nested resources. I was able to get it to work by using the secrets avm setting the parent key vault in a separate step. It seems like it passing them nested should work as well.

https://github.com/Azure/bicep-registry-modules/blob/main/avm/res/key-vault/vault/secret/main.bicep

A note about the secrets avm It would be nice if this was scope aware. I would like to set the scope to the parent keyvault rather than pass the name in.

